{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>That offered on the first day his offering.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to say <b>et korvano</b> ('his offering')? Perhaps it comes to say that he brought from his own [property] and not from that of the tribe. And according to our Sages of blessed memory (Sifrei pisqa 48) who were precise in this matter from the fact that He repeated saying for each one, ״זֶה קָרְבַּן נַחְשׁוֹן״ etc., ״זֶה קָרְבַּן נְתַנְאֵל״ — this teaches that they brought from their own — the reason He said <b>korvano</b> ('his offering') is because there are two aspects of bringing [הקרבה]: (1) the extraordinary measure of his merits and his nearness before HaShem, as stated regarding this matter in their words of blessed memory (Bemidbar Rabba 13:10) — wondrous were the deeds of Nahshon; and (2) the offering itself. Therefore He said <b>hikriv et korvano</b> ('he offered his offering') — meaning, he offered the offering spoken of in the matter, together with his hidden offering, which is the essential core of the good deed whose doing he made extraordinary. And you shall know that with a person's offering, the forces of holiness that surround him and that are attached to him are unified and joined together. Further, in that the aspect of the offering activates the bringing near of the branches of holiness, Scripture testifies that Nahshon's hand attained to offer his offering — meaning, that which needed to be offered from the sparks of holiness and their branches, he offered and unified as one everything that needed to be brought near and unified from his aspect. And this is the statement <b>ha-makriv et korvano</b> ('that offers his offering') — and this he accomplishes through power and intention in the bringing of the offering." "<b>For the tribe of Judah.</b> And it does not say 'nasi' ('prince') as is said regarding all the tribes, to say that Nahshon was worthy to offer first even though he was not a prince, and his excellence was from his own self — unlike all the other princes, whose excellence was only by virtue of their being princes of the tribe. Further, it intends to emphasize his essence, that he did not consider himself a prince but rather as one of the members of the tribe. And because we find our Sages of blessed memory (Tanhuma Bemidbar verse 48) sought to expound the names of the princes, each one according to his deeds, it appears that this righteous man's name too will declare that he was the one who was unique in volunteering to sanctify His name by descending into the sea. And this is what it says <b>Nahshon</b> — the letter nun interchanges with lamed in the letters of daTLaNaT, so that even though the nahshol (surge) of the sea surged against Israel who are called עמי (My people), he volunteered and descended into the sea, as they said of blessed memory (Sotah 37a)."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "The phrase אות נו״ן מתחלפת בלמ״ד appearing in comment 1 refers to the mnemonic דטלנ״ת, listing the letters that can interchange via the BeGeD KeFeReT rules; this has been rendered explicitly in the translation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:06:42.903890181Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}